=== Route 1: Sant Cristòfor Main – Pandao ===

[[File:Pandao ferry terminal Sant Cristofor.png|thumb|right|300px|The Pandao ferry terminal on the Península de la Barceloneta.]]

The shortest and most frequent crossing, Route 1 connects the city centre to the Pandao district on the [[Península de la Barceloneta]], directly across the bay. The 6.4 km crossing takes approximately 10 minutes and is the primary means of reaching the city centre from the peninsula, as there is no bridge or metro crossing of the bay; the alternative is [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Line 5]], which runs south and around the bay to reach downtown, a significantly longer journey. The service runs from 05:00 to 01:00, with 5-minute frequency during peak hours and 20-minute frequency off-peak, making it one of the busiest ferry routes in Lurandia by passenger volume.

=== Route 2: Sant Cristòfor Main – Illa del Portal ===

[[File:Illa del Portal ferry landing.png|thumb|right|300px|The ferry landing at Illa del Portal.]]

Route 2 serves [[Illa del Portal]], the nature reserve at the mouth of the bay. The 16.3 km crossing operates every 90 minutes between 07:00 and 19:00. Illa del Portal is home to a public park and nature reserve; the ferry is the primary means of access for visitors, as well as authorised personnel including researchers and wardens. Outside scheduled service hours, access requires prior arrangement with the nature reserve authority.

=== Route 3: Sant Cristòfor Main – Daurada ===

[[File:Daurada ferry Port Daurat.png|thumb|right|300px|The ferry terminal at Port Daurat, Illa Daurada.]]

Route 3 is the primary island service, connecting the mainland to [[Illa Daurada]] and the [[Municipalitat de les Illes]]. The 30.6 km crossing is the network's most significant route in terms of ridership, serving both the island's permanent residents (who commute to the mainland for work) and tourists visiting the island's hotels and beaches. The service runs from 05:00 to 01:00, with hourly frequency during peak hours and every 2–3 hours off-peak.

=== Route 4: Sant Cristòfor Main – Costa Nord ===

Route 4 is a limited commuter service to the Costa Nord terminal on the northern waterfront, near the border with [[Els Aiguamolls]]. The 20.6 km crossing operates only during weekday rush hours, providing an alternative to road and rail for commuters from the northern suburbs.

=== Route 5: Sant Cristòfor Main – Parc Nacional wetlands ===

[[File:Parc Nacional wetlands ferry.png|thumb|right|300px|A Parc-class monohull approaching the Parc Nacional de la Marjal wetlands terminal.]]

Route 5 is a recreational service connecting the city to the wetlands and mangrove forests of the [[Parc Nacional de la Marjal]], located on the Shendan coast north of the city. The 46.6 km route is the longest in the network and operates only on weekends, public holidays, Fridays, and Mondays, with departures every 2–3 hours between 10:00 and 21:00. The service is primarily used by day-trippers and eco-tourists visiting the national park's mangrove boardwalks, birdwatching hides, and visitor centre.

=== Route 6: Sant Cristòfor Main – Platja de Jordi ===

Route 6 serves the southern end of the [[Península de la Barcelona]], terminating at '''Platja de Jordi''', a residential neighbourhood named after the beach that once occupied the site. The beach has since been lost to coastal development and erosion, but the name persists. The 6.7 km crossing operates at high frequency during peak hours, providing a commuter service for residents of southern Barcelona. The service was introduced in 2018 to relieve pressure on Route 1 and to serve the growing residential population of the southern peninsula.

=== Route 7: Sant Cristòfor Main – El Fort ===

Route 7 serves the northern end of the [[Península de la Barcelona]], terminating at '''El Fort''', a historic fortification on the northern tip of the peninsula. The 8.6 km crossing operates at moderate frequency, primarily serving commuters from the northern peninsula neighbourhoods. The fort itself, a Zong-era coastal battery, is a minor tourist attraction, but the route's primary function is commuter transport.

=== Route O: Circular bay service ===

Route O is a circular service that loops around the Badia de Sant Cristòfor, calling at Sant Cristòfor Main, Platja de Jordi, Pandao, and El Fort before returning to Sant Cristòfor Main. The 24.5 km loop operates every 30 minutes from 06:00 to 22:00, providing a cross-peninsula connection that allows passengers to travel between Barcelona terminals without returning to the mainland hub. The service is primarily used by peninsula residents travelling between neighbourhoods and by tourists visiting the peninsula's attractions.

== Fleet ==

[[File:Badia-class and Illa-class ferries Sant Cristofor.png|thumb|right|300px|Badia-class (foreground) and Illa-class (background) vessels at Sant Cristòfor Main terminal.]]

The Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor operates a fleet of 25 vessels across seven classes, designed for the specific demands of each route.

